    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Executive Summary & Verdict\n\n**This is an outstanding, top-of-band offer for an L6 Product Manager at Google.** \n\nGiven that Google is your first choice and you do not have competing offers in hand, you should **definitely not walk away**. Your base salary is at the ~95th percentile of Google\u2019s public band ($183k\u2013$271k), the $1.34M equity grant is at the very high end for L6 (median is typically $600k\u2013$900k over 4 years), and the $200k sign-on bonus is well above the typical L6 range ($50k\u2013$100k).\n\nYou have the option to either **accept immediately** or execute a **low-risk \"closing ask\"** to see if they can round up the numbers before you sign.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Compensation Breakdown vs. Meta IC6\n\n#### A. Annual Compensation Comparison\n\n| Component | Current Meta (IC6) | Google Offer (L6) | Difference |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $260,000 | +$20,000 (+8.3%) |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $52,000 (20%) | +$16,000 (+44.4%) |\n| **Annualized Equity** | ~$250,000 | $335,000 ($1.34M / 4 yrs) | +$85,000 (+34.0%) |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | \u2014 | $200,000 | +$200,000 |\n| **Year 1 Target TC (Even Vesting)** | **~$526,000** | **$847,000** | **+$321,000 (+61.0%)** |\n\n---\n\n#### B. The Impact of Google's Front-Loaded Vesting (GSU Schedule)\n\nGoogle typically vests initial equity grants on a front-loaded schedule: **38% in Year 1, 32% in Year 2, 20% in Year 3, and 10% in Year 4** (or in some divisions, 33/33/22/12). \n\nApplying Google's standard 38/32/20/10 schedule to your $1,340,000 grant gives the following cash flow:\n\n* **Year 1:**\n  * Base: $260,000\n  * Bonus (Target): $52,000\n  * Equity (38% of $1.34M): $509,200\n  * Sign-on Bonus: $200,000\n  * **Year 1 Total Compensation: ~$1,021,200** *(nearly double your Meta compensation)*\n* **Year 2:**\n  * Base: $260,000 + Bonus: $52,000 + Equity (32% = $428,800) = **$740,800** (plus any Year 1 refresher vest)\n* **Year 3:**\n  * Base: $260,000 + Bonus: $52,000 + Equity (20% = $268,000) + refreshers = **$580,000+**\n* **Year 4:**\n  * Base: $260,000 + Bonus: $52,000 + Equity (10% = $134,000) + refreshers = **$446,000+**\n\n> **Note on Refresher Stacking:** Because Year 4 equity drops to 10%, Google awards annual performance refreshers (which vest over multiple years) to smooth out Years 3 and 4. A solid performer at L6 typically receives $100k\u2013$175k/year in refresher grants, keeping sustained annual comp above $600k\u2013$650k+.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should You Negotiate?\n\nBecause you have **no competing offers** and the offer is already at the upper boundary of the L6 band, aggressive negotiation carries downside with little room for significant movement. \n\nHowever, you can make a **one-time, low-risk closing ask**. Recruiter negotiations at this stage are routine, and making a polite, enthusiastic request that ties directly to your immediate commitment will not cause the offer to be rescinded.\n\n#### What Levers to Target:\n1. **Base Salary ($260k):** **Do not push.** The max for the posted range is $271k. $260k is already at the band ceiling.\n2. **Equity ($1.34M):** This is the best lever. You can ask to round this up to **$1.45M \u2013 $1.5M** over 4 years.\n3. **Sign-on Bonus ($200k):** Already generous, but if they cannot move equity, you can request an additional **$25k\u2013$50k** (e.g., $225k\u2013$250k) as a one-time signing incentive.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Negotiation Script / Approach\n\nContact your recruiter over the phone (or send an email asking for a quick call) using the following framing:\n\n> *\"Thank you again for putting this offer together. I\u2019m thrilled about the team, the scope of the role, and the opportunity at Google. Google is my absolute top choice, and I\u2019m ready to commit.*\n> \n> *Looking closely at my current unvested trajectory at Meta and the transition, if we can get the total equity grant to **$1,450,000** (or increase the sign-on to **$250,000**), I am ready to sign the offer letter today.*\n> \n> *Is this something you could take to the compensation committee for approval?\"*\n\n### 5. Final Takeaway\n* If they agree and bump equity or sign-on: **Sign immediately.**\n* If they hold firm and say this is their final ceiling: **Accept with enthusiasm.** The package is already top-of-market for an L6 PM in the Bay Area.",
